    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm working on my thesis chapter regarding the Maillard reaction and I'm stuck on the kinetic details. Can you explain the specific role that amino acids and reducing sugars play in the initial stages of the reaction, and how fluctuations in pH levels specifically affect the rate of non-enzymatic browning in protein-rich substrates?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "In the initial stages of the Maillard reaction, reducing sugars (such as glucose or fructose) react with the amino group of amino acids or peptides to form a stable N-substituted glycosylamine. Subsequently, this intermediate undergoes intramolecular rearrangement to form an Amadori or Heyns rearrangement product, which is the key stable species before further degradation occurs. Fluctuations in pH significantly alter this rate because low ionic strength suppresses the reaction, while alkaline conditions accelerate it by deprotonating the amino group of the protein and increasing the nucleophilicity of the carbonyl carbon of the sugar. In protein-rich substrates, however, an extremely high pH can lead to the hydrolysis of peptide bonds or protein unfolding, which may ultimately hinder the reaction kinetics despite the faster initial steps.",
